--TEST--
Core\Arr::padStart and padEnd renumber every key, where array_pad renumbers the integer keys and keeps the string ones
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`rule:types/preserve-keys` removes every rule that asks what type a key is, and array_pad's key handling is one of them: it renumbers the integer keys of the subject and keeps the string ones, then numbers the added entries into whatever integer keys are left over — so the same call answers a list or a map depending on what the subject's keys happened to be, and an added entry's key depends on how many *earlier* entries were integer-keyed. `padStart`/`padEnd` return a list whatever went in, which spec § 2 states as the rule and `nvs_core_arr_pad_start`'s doc comment argues: padding adds entries, there is no non-arbitrary key for an added one beside an existing map's, so keeping is not a choice that can be offered and the option does not exist. Two further consequences are pinned below — a subject already at or past `$size` still comes back as a list here where PHP returns it untouched, and PHP's left pad walks its own renumbering into the middle of the subject.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function of(array<int> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => int $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . "=" . $v . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// No integer key at all. `array_pad($map, 4, 0)` answers `x=1;y=2;z=3;0=0;`,
// keeping the three strings and giving the one added entry the key "0" — which
// is a key the subject could perfectly well have held already. Novis numbers the
// whole result from zero.
array<int> $map = ["x" => 1, "y" => 2, "z" => 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::padEnd($map, 4, 0)), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::padStart($map, 4, 0)), "\n";

// A subject already past `$size`: PHP returns it unchanged, keys and all, where
// Novis has already said its answer is a list of the values. Nothing is added on
// either side, so this row is entirely about the keys.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::padEnd($map, 2, 0)), "\n";

// Both kinds of key in one subject, which is where PHP's rule is at its least
// predictable. `array_pad($mixed, 5, 0)` answers `0=1;x=2;1=3;2=0;3=0;` — the
// two integer keys renumbered around a string left alone, and then the pads
// continuing that numbering. The left pad is stranger still:
// `array_pad($mixed, -5, 0)` answers `0=0;1=0;2=1;x=2;3=3;`, so the renumbering
// of the subject's own entries depends on how many entries were prepended.
array<int> $mixed = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::padEnd($mixed, 5, 0)), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::padStart($mixed, 5, 0)), "\n";

// The list case, on the same page, so the divergence is visibly about keys and
// not about which entries the member adds: over a list the two rules coincide
// and `arr-pad-end-matches-array_pad` asserts that against PHP itself.
array<int> $list = [1, 2, 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::padEnd($list, 5, 0)), "|", Render::of(Core\Arr::padStart($list, 5, 0)), "|\n";
--EXPECT--
0=1;1=2;2=3;3=0;
0=0;1=1;2=2;3=3;
0=1;1=2;2=3;
0=1;1=2;2=3;3=0;4=0;
0=0;1=0;2=1;3=2;4=3;
0=1;1=2;2=3;3=0;4=0;|0=0;1=0;2=1;3=2;4=3;|
